1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A TCP connection is two counters that must agree, one for each direction of data.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Opening the connection costs three segments, which is one round trip.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Closing costs four segments, and the side that closes first then waits 240 seconds.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Each counter is 32 bits: it runs to 4,294,967,295 and then wraps to zero.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Six control bits do the work: URG, ACK, PSH, RST, SYN and FIN, one bit each.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
SYN and FIN each occupy one sequence number; ACK occupies none.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
That single rule is why an acknowledgment of a SYN is the SYN number plus one.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Two more bits, CWR and ECE, were added later for congestion marking, making eight.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
A opens with a SYN carrying its initial number, 100, and no acknowledgment.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
B answers with SYN and ACK: its own number 300, and 101 for A's SYN.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
A replies with 101 and acknowledges 301; both sides are now ESTABLISHED.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Three segments, one round trip: 101 = 100 + 1 and 301 = 300 + 1.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Two messages would leave one thing unconfirmed: that B's number was received.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
A stale SYN from an earlier connection looks exactly like a new one.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
The third message is the only proof that A saw B's number, so B needs it.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
The cost is one round trip for the opener, and one and a half for the listener.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
The initial number is neither zero nor a plain random draw: it is a clock plus a hash.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
The clock ticks about every 4 microseconds and wraps in about 4.77 hours.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
One maximum segment lifetime, 120 seconds, spans 30 million ticks of that clock.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Zero would collide with an old connection's numbering, which is the whole problem.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
The opening segments carry 20 bytes of options; the fixed header is 20, so 40 in all.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
MSS is 4 bytes, SACK-permitted 2, timestamps 10, a NOP and window scale 3.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Options are legal on a SYN; the other segments carry none by default.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
No MSS option means 536 bytes over IPv4 and 1220 over IPv6.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
A sends 1000 bytes, then a FIN at 1101, which costs one number.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
B acknowledges 1102 and enters CLOSE-WAIT; A is in FIN-WAIT-2.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
B's FIN at 301 is acknowledged with 302, and A enters TIME-WAIT.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Four segments: FIN, ACK, FIN, ACK, with one sequence number for each FIN.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
TIME-WAIT lasts twice the maximum segment lifetime: 2 x 120 is 240 seconds.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Reason one: if the last ACK is lost, the peer resends its FIN and it is answered.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Reason two: delayed duplicates from this connection die before the pair is reused.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
A host may reopen from TIME-WAIT only with a larger initial number.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Simultaneous open: both send SYN, both answer SYN and ACK, four segments.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Both go CLOSED to SYN-SENT to SYN-RECEIVED to ESTABLISHED.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Simultaneous close leaves both sides in TIME-WAIT, so two sockets wait, not one.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Half-close: CLOSE-WAIT has no timer, so the other side may keep sending.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
First the flags: SYN and FIN cost one number each, ACK costs none.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Then the opening: 101 = 100 + 1 and 301 = 300 + 1, in three segments.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Then the close: 1102 = 1101 + 1 and 302 = 301 + 1, then 240 seconds of waiting.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Six slips: a numbered ACK, a FIN that costs nothing, a zero start, options off the SYN, one-sided close, and no wait.

